From c68de4692af966fc66371bf814d9b0f962d0cc30 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 05:03:06 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Keep the queues alive while surfaces still refer to them

Every seek dropped hardware decoding permanently. On the first
re-initialisation the client would report

  h264: Failed to create decode context: 1 (operation failed)
  Failed setup for format vaapi: hwaccel initialisation returned error
  Using software decoding

and never recover, which on a looping file shows up as a flicker partway
through and a picture that quietly changes character.

RequestDestroyContext released both queues and cleared video_format
unconditionally. Destroying a context is not on its own a statement that the
surfaces are finished with, and a client is entitled to keep its surface pool
across a decoder re-initialisation. FFmpeg's does: mpv caches the frame pool and
reuses it whenever format, sw_format, size and pool depth are unchanged -- which
is exactly the case on a loop or a seek -- so the CONTEXT is destroyed and
recreated while the SURFACES live on. RequestCreateContext opens by reading
video_format, found it NULL, and refused.

Apply the guard RequestDestroySurfaces already has: release the queues only once
neither a surface nor a context refers to them.

Reproduced and fixed independently of the scaler work. A 720p file, which asks
for no scaling at all, fails identically, so this is not a consequence of
decode-time downscaling.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/context.c | 20 ++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 20 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/context.c b/src/context.c
index 5f59db3..b8aa332 100644
--- a/src/context.c
+++ b/src/context.c
@@ -170,6 +170,7 @@ VAStatus RequestDestroyContext(VADriverContextP context, VAContextID context_id)
 	struct video_format *video_format;
 	unsigned int output_type, capture_type;
 	VAStatus status;
+	int iterator;
 	int rc;
 
 	video_format = driver_data->video_format;
@@ -203,6 +204,25 @@ VAStatus RequestDestroyContext(VADriverContextP context, VAContextID context_id)
 	object_heap_free(&driver_data->context_heap,
 			 (struct object_base *)context_object);
 
+	/*
+	 * Release the queues only once nothing refers to them any more, exactly
+	 * as RequestDestroySurfaces does. Destroying a context is not on its own
+	 * a statement that the surfaces are finished with.
+	 *
+	 * Unconditionally freeing the buffers here and clearing video_format
+	 * broke every seek. A client is entitled to keep its surface pool across
+	 * a decoder re-initialisation, and FFmpeg's does: mpv caches the frame
+	 * pool and reuses it whenever the format and size are unchanged, so a
+	 * loop or a seek destroys and recreates the CONTEXT while the SURFACES
+	 * live on. RequestCreateContext then found video_format NULL and
+	 * refused, which surfaces to the user as
+	 * "Failed to create decode context: 1 (operation failed)", a permanent
+	 * drop to software decoding, and a visible flicker mid-playback.
+	 */
+	if (object_heap_first(&driver_data->surface_heap, &iterator) != NULL ||
+	    object_heap_first(&driver_data->context_heap, &iterator) != NULL)
+		return VA_STATUS_SUCCESS;
+
 	rc = v4l2_request_buffers(driver_data->video_fd, output_type, 0);
 	if (rc < 0)
 		return VA_STATUS_ERROR_OPERATION_FAILED;
-- 
